    {"id": 155, "source_en": "If you're stuck on one question in an exam, move on and come back — don't burn the clock on a single problem.", "category": "education", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 156, "source_en": "Show your working in maths even if the final answer is wrong — examiners give partial credit for the right method.", "category": "education", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 157, "source_en": "Always read the marking rubric before you start writing — it tells you exactly what gets marks.", "category": "education", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 158, "source_en": "Reflect after each exam: what worked, what didn't, what you'd prepare differently next time.", "category": "education", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 159, "source_en": "A small daily study habit beats a panicked cramming session the week before an exam.", "category": "education", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 160, "source_en": "Lecturers usually drop hints in class about what's coming on the exam — pay attention to what they repeat.", "category": "education", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 161, "source_en": "The English word appreciate usually translates better as thank deeply or value depending on the context.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 162, "source_en": "In English, please is often softer than the direct equivalents in many African languages — it's mostly a politeness marker.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 163, "source_en": "Try saying it in your own language first, then translating word by word — direct translation often loses the meaning.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 164, "source_en": "The word manager in English covers what other languages might split into boss, supervisor, and team lead.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 165, "source_en": "English has no separate formal you — it's the same word whether you're speaking to a child or an elder.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 166, "source_en": "When translating a CV or formal letter, prefer the more formal English word — commenced rather than started.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 167, "source_en": "English idioms rarely translate literally — it's raining cats and dogs means heavy rain, not animals falling from the sky.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 168, "source_en": "The phrase to take a rain check means to postpone something — useful in informal English, easy to misread.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 169, "source_en": "In a job application, I look forward to hearing from you is a standard closing — not actually about hearing anything.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 170, "source_en": "Kindly in English emails is usually polite, but in over-use it can sound passive-aggressive — use sparingly.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 171, "source_en": "The English I'd love to usually means I'd like to — it's much weaker than the literal romantic meaning suggests.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 172, "source_en": "I'm fine in English often means the opposite, depending on the tone — context matters more than the words.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 173, "source_en": "Translating between English and Oshiwambo requires unpacking ideas — Oshiwambo often packs whole sentences into one word.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 174, "source_en": "When someone says let me think about it in English, they may mean I'm politely declining rather than literally considering.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 175, "source_en": "The English present continuous is used for things happening right now — many languages use the simple present for this.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 176, "source_en": "English could you is more polite than can you — the conditional softens the request even though the meaning is the same.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 177, "source_en": "The word sorry in English does double duty — it apologises and expresses sympathy without admitting fault.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 178, "source_en": "Tomorrow in spoken English sometimes means soon rather than literally the next day — context decides.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 179, "source_en": "The English I'm afraid before bad news isn't about fear — it's a polite softener, like unfortunately.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 180, "source_en": "When translating to English from any language, read your translation out loud — if it sounds awkward in English, it probably is.", "category": "translation", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 181, "source_en": "For a fever lasting more than two days in Namibia, especially in the northern regions, please consider a malaria test.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 182, "source_en": "Stay hydrated through the hot months — small frequent sips are easier on the body than gulping a whole bottle at once.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 183, "source_en": "Before borrowing, calculate what you'll actually pay back over the full loan term — interest adds up faster than people expect.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 184, "source_en": "Save first, spend what's left — it sounds simple but most budgets fail because people do it the other way around.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 185, "source_en": "Disagreements between parents and adult children are often less about the topic and more about feeling unheard.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 186, "source_en": "Looking after an ageing parent is hard work — make sure you also have someone looking out for you.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 187, "source_en": "For most government applications in Namibia, bring a certified copy of your ID rather than the original.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 188, "source_en": "Public service offices are usually quieter mid-week and mid-morning — try Tuesday or Wednesday around 10am.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 189, "source_en": "If your phone is running slow, restarting it once a day usually helps more than people expect.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
    {"id": 190, "source_en": "Be careful with apps that ask for your bank login or ID number — never share those outside the official bank app.", "category": "niche", "source_origin": "crafted"},
